#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133878 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133694 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "234800a9-ae9c-56c4-8284-84041f129a59" :question/math-academy-id "e-8759" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+ 2xz.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle e^{t}, t, e^{-t}\\rangle$ for $t\\in \\left[0,1\\right],$ evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨e,1,\\frac{1}{e}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,1)$ and $\\left(e, 1, \\dfrac{1}{e}\\right).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e,1,\\frac{1}{e})-f(1,0,1) \\\\  & =(e(1)^{2}+2\\cdot e\\cdot \\frac{1}{e})-(1(0)^{2}+2(1)(1)) \\\\  & =(e+2)-2 \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538149 :question-generator/id #uuid "c0f94114-d881-5eba-937b-16347aa0a14c" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186133878.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00980506-979a-5a3c-bb6b-3b50f009bfac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415067} {:answer/id #uuid "1aeef040-2421-5b1c-b55a-b92ee77dadcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415070} {:answer/id #uuid "5daedb86-7dd6-576d-aff0-8f7b1f0062ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415078} {:answer/id #uuid "a2214f1f-3631-5be2-85e4-d5102a1c44c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415083} {:answer/id #uuid "d6de32bd-280a-5c5a-aa34-19f1b3faadf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5daedb86-7dd6-576d-aff0-8f7b1f0062ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415078} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d412271-dad8-50a6-a5b4-2d3051bb421a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63f96647-0e17-5269-a429-2d3bdcfb1cff" :question/math-academy-id "q-104616" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨3t,{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(3,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(3,1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({3}^{3}+1)-({0}^{3}+0) \\\\  & =(27+1)-0 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f63d9a1-0137-5a36-bb60-9969b4573aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360969} {:answer/id #uuid "3699a6b4-d4d9-5e31-b162-16c5007ee57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361083} {:answer/id #uuid "89c4b85e-187b-55d0-84b5-4705fe0ffb20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361449} {:answer/id #uuid "be1533c1-05ff-53d2-b2f2-b8184c361484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361686} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0d25c8-b49e-5a74-a54a-3eb3215a8c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3699a6b4-d4d9-5e31-b162-16c5007ee57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361083} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f54e0bb-f312-5428-9eb3-1323f1fab514" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident