" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238153} {:answer/id #uuid "44bbd131-4ecd-540b-ad14-259cefa4dcf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238651} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab4b784-0b5f-5e1b-9fde-ec17a7e2873d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238701} {:answer/id #uuid "89f290a7-499e-5b23-8f65-ac96513f25dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239255} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb83a5e-cf58-56d7-bc9a-928b68a83802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecb83a5e-cf58-56d7-bc9a-928b68a83802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240135} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbad1630-befd-5b0d-9389-d98b46343814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1eb7495c-4bb1-5f06-bc1d-e458481de613" :question/math-academy-id "q-104623" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}\\cdot \\pi )-({0}^{2}\\cdot 0-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =(2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e699747-9682-50ad-bc3c-89217394ac15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238310} {:answer/id #uuid "2607fd46-ddb8-5e68-96ad-e64aed38f812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238385} {:answer/id #uuid "897eb260-f9ce-5892-bb70-7962baeb2fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239250} {:answer/id #uuid "95fcd466-856f-5f24-95dd-68c6e3fb2527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239353} {:answer/id #uuid "e5429a9a-ce6f-502f-af4d-a0421c2ce2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95fcd466-856f-5f24-95dd-68c6e3fb2527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239353} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c7cb5bc-2de6-517b-a738-225b68f34e76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "377033ea-e89e-5b49-a8b3-206ce2c51bb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104640" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(2\\pi ) & =⟨1,0,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,0)$ and $(1,0,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,0,2\\pi )-f(1,0,0) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 2\\pi +1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0)-({1}^{2}\\cdot 0+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(2\\pi +0)-0 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302b8581-4a68-5c5c-8795-9744e9d4a2d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257206} {:answer/id #uuid "953e0310-1f6a-5dcb-9428-9966108f8f1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258094} {:answer/id #uuid "b72e2f4d-4041-5680-a701-550b3147bc12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258378} {:answer/id #uuid "ca82cadd-75e5-598b-bacc-17ec432f7824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258541} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b95c82-3200-5da8-b666-432aa9c1aa8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "106" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5b95c82-3200-5da8-b666-432aa9c1aa8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "106" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258928} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2dc29a-3749-5531-9860-c3324dac8a28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "003b8481-988f-56eb-a4c6-1362503d86f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-124002" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t},t+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ r(4) & =⟨2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,5).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,5)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({2}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}+2+5)-({0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+0+1) \\\\  & =107-1 \\\\  & =106.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eea1d61-c31a-52a0-9c80-b705eeabdf54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256925} {:answer/id #uuid "32a24c80-640c-5f96-9d30-3886433c358a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257225} {:answer/id #uuid "89fe846b-c77b-5114-acc8-ae328a43149b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257994} {:answer/id #uuid "d080e35c-13c8-5910-bdbc-1433606170a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258595} {:answer/id #uuid "de2288dd-737a-5b6e-99cf-5d7174075a26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d080e35c-13c8-5910-bdbc-1433606170a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258595} :answer-field/id #uuid "13ff9e85-14c9-5103-9111-1d260075e876" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c23e8787-68ef-58ab-bf57-8f769ed64353" :question/math-academy-id "q-124003" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{e}^{2x}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,3{t}^{2}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(3{e}^{2\\cdot 1})-(0\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb8367f-a159-5457-99f5-50133f64eb7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256893} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa182ef-4a0a-5db2-a08b-55a80052cb00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256930} {:answer/id #uuid "228efe5e-c3c4-564d-9dab-7a9a4edeb67d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257068} {:answer/id #uuid "3b05dbc7-a00e-579d-9183-316aebb35c95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257306} {:answer/id #uuid "4e044364-8237-5de2-8e2f-2b3e611819bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e044364-8237-5de2-8e2f-2b3e611819bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257452} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e3f4676-5218-53c9-a375-46cf12254dde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "615b0135-e131-5b5f-9638-4296b1aa2a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-124005" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ) \\\\  & =(2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\pi )-(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "543ba384-51b6-557c-bf16-43ac82ca0e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257502} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd16522-1981-538e-a666-611647c995ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258455} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c6cd51-6e8f-5103-b8e1-9cf9c4743823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258755} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc320e3-b118-5e6b-bdc4-5e336a48b133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258824} {:answer/id #uuid "f7bffceb-ddbe-55f4-ac78-cfd9d1162748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2c6cd51-6e8f-5103-b8e1-9cf9c4743823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258755} :answer-field/id #uuid "63cb2a76-38cc-5ba0-833b-b7d3644b3675" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26e83fe0-4239-5444-b048-589ba5747a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-124008" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+{e}^{z}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2},2{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,1,2)-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =({e}^{1}+{e}^{1}+{e}^{2})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(2e+{e}^{2})-3 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2e-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c8a508-1d4a-574f-bb45-6efb1ced71fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415068} {:answer/id #uuid "47d59bce-11d1-59ca-a980-8662920cfd55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415076} {:answer/id #uuid "cdbbdd8b-85ea-5741-9623-bb79a02c5a1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415090} {:answer/id #uuid "d992fc1d-6425-5951-9f5f-b0d131ab532a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415094} {:answer/id #uuid "e72ad3e5-d9a9-5e06-b65e-7fe892762e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdbbdd8b-85ea-5741-9623-bb79a02c5a1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415090} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aa64add-60f3-564a-9626-a2687efca2f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03426578-7377-543b-bd88-93276fa901ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-104638" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,{t}^{2}-1,{t}^{2}-2t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨5,3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,-1,0)$ and $(5,3,0).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(5,3,0)-f(1,-1,0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {e}^{5(0)}-(-1)\\cdot {e}^{1(0)} \\\\  & =3+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12811091-da24-5f9e-a3cc-d521c187f782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415069} {:answer/id #uuid "48539c5b-fd63-583d-8da2-566a4dd6e0bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415077} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d2cb1e-5c01-5454-b9f6-ddf29ce394b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415082} {:answer/id #uuid "b864bbf5-46ca-5a26-aa58-751cd0107a9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415087} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf966f8-fa1b-5eaf-ab84-b21f9b032f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdf966f8-fa1b-5eaf-ab84-b21f9b032f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415091} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab184202-13b3-5378-a512-5889441d45af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e8a51b9-ad21-56cd-a267-db39e81294ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-104624" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y+{x}^{2}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,{t}^{2}-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(4,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(4,3)-f(0,-1) \\\\  & =(3+{4}^{2})-(-1+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3+16)-(-1) \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ceeb53b-391b-5fef-9ab5-1097ef938825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415073} {:answer/id #uuid "2f083f8e-a1a4-56c3-aea6-e98468b6122b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415074} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b875c4-e194-5a11-823c-e0a27f17aa6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415086} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5489e8-7985-5f2a-b47e-2c5f4e8a4b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415089} {:answer/id #uuid "d782c18b-724f-503c-9bd1-3a2cfd6e6827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f083f8e-a1a4-56c3-aea6-e98468b6122b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415074} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6b783c0-b546-54f4-ac0c-0b114a515648" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a59364c1-7975-54ce-83e7-b32f8ae82486" :question/math-academy-id "q-104639" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{4},{t}^{6}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,1,1)-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{3})-(0\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{3}) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6f935a-7227-56de-9d74-d58f3261b90a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415071} {:answer/id #uuid "376dde01-d46a-5414-9e84-a7d099ab2f33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415075} {:answer/id #uuid "75de655b-bac7-5de2-9e4c-ce56729e4a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415080} {:answer/id #uuid "87f317ce-1ebd-53fe-bd1c-2786b6ebd37d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415081} {:answer/id #uuid "db14b874-47ec-5b46-a18d-4f53face6c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "376dde01-d46a-5414-9e84-a7d099ab2f33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415075} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fdf3bea-7332-5701-aa59-1894b6d9cc82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3d23b94-95d1-5af0-9020-b1e5d5ab8286" :question/math-academy-id "q-104637" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{2}{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $(0,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f((r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(0,2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{0(2)}-0\\cdot (1)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{1(0)} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15b11761-2ef2-5ceb-a8c8-ffbe832809e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{e^{2}} + 50 e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538110} {:answer/id #uuid "5550ce8b-a330-5d05-a7e2-450b1c4c0130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538120} {:answer/id #uuid "888723f5-95e9-5497-a702-b81d6ee6aed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 50 e^{2} - \\frac{10}{e^{2}} + 24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538129} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7fcd3b-6226-5771-b4d1-350798052ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24 + \\frac{10}{e^{2}} + 50 e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538130} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9f8939-8a29-5353-a94a-d5c6161080f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{e^{2}} + 24 + 50 e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d7fcd3b-6226-5771-b4d1-350798052ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24 + \\frac{10}{e^{2}} + 50 e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538130} :answer-field/id #uuid "9522aaa5-9e4d-58cf-88b3-a6672f006add" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "10952efb-f91e-5efa-8836-2bdfa5e4547d" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=2 x y^{2} + 2 y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 3,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨e^{2},5,e^{-2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,3,1)$ and $(e^{2},5,e^{-2}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e^{2},5,e^{-2})-f(1,3,1) \\\\  & =\\left(2 \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(5\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(5\\right) \\left(e^{-2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(3\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(3\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{10}{e^{2}} + 50 e^{2}\\right)-\\left(24\\right) \\\\  & =-24 + \\frac{10}{e^{2}} + 50 e^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a2220e-715f-57f8-8743-057e266ca318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 4 e - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538114} {:answer/id #uuid "48fd3aa2-5c7d-540a-8ffd-5be89dfb1429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 + 4 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538117} {:answer/id #uuid "6546aaf9-7cff-56ea-b59f-85e4aeb74c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 + 4 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538124} {:answer/id #uuid "9c99bae7-2dc2-5b64-885b-d754ce59bea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538133} {:answer/id #uuid "cd403008-fbc5-583c-96a7-a0c78f2b027d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 + 4 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd403008-fbc5-583c-96a7-a0c78f2b027d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 + 4 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538140} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c5d2f31-c569-5ce6-b4d2-c54ef953da59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55b9003c-2f9b-5f89-8acf-67ac21d03597" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=x y^{2} + 3 x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 1,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨e,2,e^{-1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,1,1)$ and $(e,2,e^{-1}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e,2,e^{-1})-f(1,1,1) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(e\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2} + 3 \\left(e\\right) \\left(2\\right) \\left(e^{-1}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)^{2} + 3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(6 + 4 e\\right)-\\left(4\\right) \\\\  & =2 + 4 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1557e0a8-d585-523d-babf-b225f2b9a2ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 - 25 e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538109} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9fe5a3-cec7-5200-87f9-a77a6de16f45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 25 e^{2} - 25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538115} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa93bc4-860e-5e12-a31b-4f116ce4d740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 25 e^{2} - 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538131} {:answer/id #uuid "af665cd1-c2e0-5c16-bd26-b9766458bd3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538135} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7a2ada-2d8c-5325-a3ef-40ae29016e2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5 + 25 e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1557e0a8-d585-523d-babf-b225f2b9a2ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 - 25 e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538109} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dbbff86-e68c-5eab-90c3-61a3fcbc667f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f288c44-ccaa-54db-bd90-19654202c121" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=- x y^{2} - 2 x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 3,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨e^{2},5,e^{-2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,3,1)$ and $(e^{2},5,e^{-2}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e^{2},5,e^{-2})-f(1,3,1) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(5\\right)^{2} - 2 \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(5\\right) \\left(e^{-2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(- \\left(1\\right) \\left(3\\right)^{2} - 2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(3\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(- 25 e^{2} - 10\\right)-\\left(-15\\right) \\\\  & =5 - 25 e^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5674d226-aed0-5af3-94c0-c08de387fb42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 54 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538123} {:answer/id #uuid "b6677f9e-c46e-5df1-a022-79ff79ead6b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 216 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538136} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb35463-fe83-57c2-9aa8-404bf6c9df5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "108 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538138} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ded7cd-20cb-515e-90ac-79e2da37e37b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538144} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c73618-b299-5c5a-8259-3519762335fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 108 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9c73618-b299-5c5a-8259-3519762335fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 108 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538147} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa67956c-515d-580e-8ab9-dbe6238b9841" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb2e2e55-8936-54d7-a33c-03469d398524" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x^{2} z - 2 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\sin{\\left(t \\right)},3 t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨3,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(2 \\pi) & =⟨3,0,6 \\pi⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(3,0,0)$ and $(3,0,6 \\pi).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2 \\pi))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(3,0,6 \\pi)-f(3,0,0) \\\\  & =\\left(- 2 \\left(3\\right)^{2} \\left(6 \\pi\\right) - 2 \\left(3\\right) \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\right)-\\left(- 2 \\left(3\\right)^{2} \\left(0\\right) - 2 \\left(3\\right) \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\left(\\left(54 \\pi+0\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =- 108 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b122fb-d082-5051-bb26-2b9c0fcc8dda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 - 8 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538108} {:answer/id #uuid "169cc0df-0d15-5b42-b984-d54bb015c91d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - 8 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538112} {:answer/id #uuid "4a663d34-c8ed-50d1-aa09-4f141042888e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 - 8 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538118} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6d4e79-214a-5156-9718-c26c48758f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3 + 8 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538125} {:answer/id #uuid "da58d612-7787-54cd-85a7-f3f5f020e812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a663d34-c8ed-50d1-aa09-4f141042888e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 - 8 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538118} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a696714-c0a6-58d5-a9b9-4ec27d224ce0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cd1bb89c-9ca9-5d27-95e0-43a9165543a7" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x y^{2} + x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 1,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨e,2,e^{-1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,1,1)$ and $(e,2,e^{-1}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e,2,e^{-1})-f(1,1,1) \\\\  & =\\left(- 2 \\left(e\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2} + \\left(e\\right) \\left(2\\right) \\left(e^{-1}\\right)\\right)-\\left(- 2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)^{2} + \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(2 - 8 e\\right)-\\left(-1\\right) \\\\  & =3 - 8 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16055ec6-dd35-5ae8-b9b2-8fae66b219d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18 + 48 e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538111} {:answer/id #uuid "29033b82-e62d-5eff-869f-6e3c77aa79fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6 + 48 e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538113} {:answer/id #uuid "557cbc89-6a9c-59ae-af39-2498105ed609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12 + 48 e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538121} {:answer/id #uuid "d22d355c-0046-5ecf-842d-6e656cb61b45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 - 48 e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538141} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b76274-e3bf-518f-9866-77422b19ef84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16055ec6-dd35-5ae8-b9b2-8fae66b219d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18 + 48 e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538111} :answer-field/id #uuid "50f558ba-5ac1-5f2d-94f2-8f408ce11078" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "edc4ddd0-1255-518c-9176-4cb2e8db04ff" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=3 x y^{2} - 3 x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 2,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨e^{2},4,e^{-2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,2,1)$ and $(e^{2},4,e^{-2}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e^{2},4,e^{-2})-f(1,2,1) \\\\  & =\\left(3 \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(4\\right)^{2} - 3 \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(4\\right) \\left(e^{-2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2} - 3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(2\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(-12 + 48 e^{2}\\right)-\\left(6\\right) \\\\  & =-18 + 48 e^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}]}
